/target
# Cargo.lock is tracked deliberately: this workspace ships a binary, so the
# lockfile is what makes a build reproducible. Together with
# rust-toolchain.toml it pins both halves — compiler and dependencies — so a
# dependency release cannot redden an unrelated pull request.
.DS_Store
*.swp
*~
.patina_history

# Benchmark reports - tracked for baseline history
# /benchmark_reports

# Larceny suite per-suite logs (regenerated by scripts/run_larceny_tests.sh;
# the summary reports beside them are tracked)
scheme_tests/reports/larceny/
scheme_tests/reports/larceny_r6rs/
scheme_tests/reports/larceny_tree_walker/
scheme_tests/reports/larceny_r6rs_tree_walker/

# SRFI 64 writes <suite-name>.log beside the process's cwd. Running a
# tests/scheme/ file by hand -- which is how each one is checked against chibi
# and Gauche -- drops one in the repo root, so ignore those.
#
# Anchored to the root deliberately. The driver's cwd is the *crate* root, and
# it installs `test-runner-null` precisely so no log is written; a
# crates/patina-tests/<suite>.log therefore means that install regressed. That
# one should stay visible in `git status` rather than be ignored with the rest.
/*.log
# Running a suite file from its own directory -- the other natural way to check
# it against an oracle -- drops the log there instead. That one carries no
# signal, so ignore it without weakening the rule above.
crates/patina-tests/tests/scheme/**/*.log
